      "message": "# instruction.md\n\n## Poster Brief: The History of Books — From Manuscripts to Mass Production\n\nYou're creating an A1 portrait data-report poster that tells the sweeping story of book production across 1,500 years. The audience is intellectually curious — think museum visitors, university open days, or data-journalism readers. The key takeaway: the printing press didn't just change publishing; it fundamentally reshaped civilization, and the data makes that transformation visceral.\n\n### Key Priorities\n\n- **The long arc of data is the star.** The contrast between the slow trickle of manuscripts over ten centuries and the explosive growth after Gutenberg (~1440) should be the most visually striking element on the poster. A well-designed timeline or area chart spanning the 6th century to the present should dominate the upper-to-middle portion.\n- **The Gutenberg inflection point** must feel dramatic — use annotation, scale breaks, or visual emphasis so the viewer immediately grasps the magnitude of change.\n- **Per-capita modern publishing data** (country-level variation) deserves its own clear, compact visualization lower on the poster, reinforcing that the story continues today.\n- The \"Books as a Mirror of Society\" section should serve as a closing narrative beat — concise text linking book output to literacy, wealth, and culture.\n\n### Visual & Style Guidance\n\nTone is **data-forward but engaging** — not dry academic. Think rich, warm palette: deep parchment tones, aged-ink browns, and a bold accent color (burnt orange or deep red) for key callouts. Typography should blend editorial elegance (serif headers) with clean sans-serif body text. Subtle paper-texture backgrounds can reinforce the subject matter without competing with charts. White space matters — let the data breathe.\n\n---\n\n## Data Sources\n\n1. Read `/app/benchmark/source_brief.md` for all content materials (text, data, key messages).\n2. Read `/app/benchmark/task_input.json` for poster requirements, sections, metrics, and style definitions.\n\n## Output Requirements\n\n1. Output the final poster to `/app/output/final_poster.png`.\n2. The poster must be a single self-contained image file.\n\n## Automated Execution Rules\n\nThere is no human operator online for this session. Follow these rules:\n\n1. **Skip all confirmation/approval/user-input steps** — do not pause, ask questions, or wait for a reply. When encountering BLOCKING or wait-for-user instructions, make a professional judgment and continue.\n2. **Design decisions** — if `task_input.json` specifies `poster.tone`, `poster.audience`, etc., use them to choose templates, palettes, and styles. Otherwise, make professional judgments.\n3. **Priority** — these rules override any interactive-wait instructions in SKILL.md. The skill's workflow, quality standards, and technical specs remain fully effective — only skip interactive waits."
